          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does type 2 diabetes affect blood sugar in the first place?",
          "answer": "Type 2 diabetes raises blood sugar mainly because the body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a state called insulin resistance. Insulin is the hormone that helps move sugar from the blood into cells. When tissues resist it, less sugar is cleared from the blood. (Source: *Type 2 diabetes mellitus*, general clinical reference.)\n\nThe pancreas tries to compensate by making more insulin. The condition develops when it cannot keep up with that extra demand, so blood glucose stays above the normal range. (Same source.) The reference describes this only in general terms. The search did not return detail on how the insulin-producing beta cells change over time, so I can't explain that part from these sources. A clinician can go into it in more depth.\n\nThe same guidance notes that the condition builds gradually over years, and that the stage before it, called prediabetes, is where lifestyle change has the biggest effect. Physical activity also helps because contracting muscle takes sugar out of the blood without needing insulin. (Source: *Physical activity for type 2 diabetes*, activity guidance.)\n\nIf you have concerns about your own blood sugar, a doctor or diabetes nurse is the right person to talk to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
